faqs:
  - question: "Which new OAuth2 providers does Appwrite support?"
    answer: "Appwrite Auth added six OAuth2 providers: Hugging Face, Resend, Cloudflare, TikTok, Kakao, and Webflow. They work with the same [OAuth2 login](/docs/products/auth/oauth2) flow as GitHub or Google. Enable them in the Appwrite Console under **Auth** > **Settings** with the client ID and secret from each provider's developer portal."
  - question: "What are the OAuthProvider enum values for the new providers?"
    answer: "They are `OAuthProvider.Huggingface`, `OAuthProvider.Resend`, `OAuthProvider.Cloudflare`, `OAuthProvider.Tiktok`, `OAuthProvider.Kakao`, and `OAuthProvider.Webflow`. Note the casing on `Huggingface` and `Tiktok`. All six are available from `node-appwrite` 29.4.0 and the `appwrite` Web SDK 28.3.0."
  - question: "Why don't users who sign in with TikTok or Resend have an email address?"
    answer: "Neither provider shares the account's email address over OAuth2. TikTok has no email scope, and Resend only exposes an account ID. Appwrite creates these users without an email and recognizes them on later sign-ins by their provider user ID. If your app needs an email, collect it after sign-in and set it from your server with the Users API."
  - question: "Why does Cloudflare or Webflow sign-in fail for a user who already has an account?"
    answer: "Cloudflare and Webflow return an email address but don't say whether it's verified. Appwrite won't sign someone into an existing account based on an unverified email, so it redirects to your failure URL when the address already belongs to another user. To connect the provider to that account, have the user sign in first and then start `createOAuth2Session` from the browser, which links the provider as a new [identity](/docs/products/auth/identities)."
  - question: "Which scopes do I need for Resend OAuth2 login?"
    answer: "Resend has no identity-only scope, so pass one of its two scopes in the `scopes` parameter: `emails:send` to send email from the user's account, or `full_access` for the rest of the Resend API. Request the narrowest one your app needs."
  - question: "Why does Kakao sign-in fail with error KOE205?"
    answer: "Kakao rejects the authorization with `KOE205` when you request a scope that isn't enabled as a consent item on your Kakao app. Appwrite requests no scopes by default, so Kakao asks for the consent items you configured. Enable a consent item in the Kakao developer console before you pass it in `scopes`."
  - question: "Are the new OAuth2 providers available on self-hosted Appwrite?"
    answer: "Yes. Hugging Face, Resend, and Cloudflare ship in self-hosted Appwrite 2.0, TikTok and Kakao in 2.1, and Webflow in 2.4. Follow the [upgrade guide](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/production/updates) to update your instance."
---

Sign-in buttons convert best when they match the accounts your users already have. The users of a model playground have Hugging Face accounts. A creator analytics tool's users are on TikTok. An app for the Korean market needs Kakao, because KakaoTalk is the default messaging app in South Korea.

Until recently, none of these were OAuth2 providers in Appwrite. Supporting one meant building your own OAuth2 client: the authorization redirect, the code exchange, PKCE where the provider requires it, token refresh, and mapping the provider's profile onto a user. Then you maintained it every time the provider changed its API.

Today, we are announcing **six new OAuth2 login providers** in Appwrite Auth: **Hugging Face**, **Resend**, **Cloudflare**, **TikTok**, **Kakao**, and **Webflow**. Each works with the same [OAuth2 login](/docs/products/auth/oauth2) flow as the providers you already use, and each has its own value in the `OAuthProvider` enum.

# What the new OAuth2 providers give you

- **Six new sign-in options.** Enable any of them in the Console and add a button. There's no OAuth2 client to build or maintain.
- **One API for every provider.** `createOAuth2Token` on the server and `createOAuth2Session` on the client work the same way for all six. Only the `OAuthProvider` value changes.
- **PKCE handled for you.** Cloudflare and Resend require PKCE. Appwrite generates the verifier and challenge and sends them during the code exchange.
- **Access tokens for each provider's API.** The session stores the provider's access token as `providerAccessToken`, so your server can call the Hugging Face, Cloudflare, Resend, or Webflow API on the user's behalf.
- **Token refresh where the provider supports it.** Appwrite refreshes the provider token through [`updateSession`](/docs/products/auth/oauth2#refresh-tokens) for every new provider except Webflow, which doesn't issue refresh tokens.
- **Linked accounts.** A user who signs in with TikTok can link Kakao or GitHub to the same account through [Identities](/docs/products/auth/identities).
- **Available everywhere.** All six are live on [Appwrite Cloud](/sign-in) today. On self-hosted Appwrite, Hugging Face, Resend, and Cloudflare ship in 2.0, TikTok and Kakao in 2.1, and Webflow in 2.4.

# Meet the six new providers

The new providers fall into two groups, and most apps need one or the other.

- **Sign-in for an audience.** TikTok and Kakao bring consumer audiences, and Kakao is the account most users in South Korea already have. Hugging Face brings AI developers and researchers. Add these when the goal is fewer abandoned sign-ups.
- **Sign-in that doubles as API access.** Resend, Cloudflare, and Webflow fit apps built on top of the user's account on that platform. The same OAuth2 flow signs the user in and gives your server a token to act on their account, so you don't need a separate "connect your account" step. Hugging Face fits here too when you request Hub scopes.

You can offer as many providers as you like side by side. Users who start with one can link the others later, and they keep a single Appwrite account.

Each provider also shares a different slice of the user's profile. Here's what Appwrite requests by default and what it stores on the user.

## Hugging Face

[Hugging Face](https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/oauth) hosts the models, datasets, and Spaces that much of the open source AI community builds on. If your app sits on top of the Hub, such as a model playground, a fine-tuning dashboard, or a tool for Spaces, your users already have a Hugging Face account.

Appwrite signs users in with Hugging Face's OpenID Connect scopes (`openid`, `profile`, and `email`) and stores their name, email, and profile photo. The email is marked as verified only when Hugging Face reports it as verified. To call the Hub on the user's behalf, add scopes such as `read-repos` or `inference-api` and use the session's `providerAccessToken`.

## Resend

[Resend](https://resend.com/docs/guides/building-a-resend-oauth-client) is an email API for developers. Sign in with Resend fits products that send email through their users' own Resend accounts, like a newsletter tool or a transactional email builder.

Resend has no identity-only scope, so you choose the access you need: `emails:send` to send email, or `full_access` for the rest of the API. Resend doesn't share the account's email address or name over OAuth2, so Appwrite identifies the user by their Resend account ID and creates the user without an email. Appwrite handles the PKCE exchange that Resend requires.

## Cloudflare

[Cloudflare OAuth](https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/oauth/) lets third-party apps sign users in with their Cloudflare account. Tools that manage Workers, DNS records, or other Cloudflare resources can now sign users in with the same account they're about to manage.

Appwrite requests `user-details.read` for the user's name and email, plus `offline_access` so the session gets a refresh token. Cloudflare requires PKCE, which Appwrite handles. Cloudflare doesn't report whether the email is verified, so Appwrite stores it as unverified.

## TikTok

[TikTok Login Kit](https://developers.tiktok.com/doc/login-kit-web) signs users in with their TikTok account. It's a natural fit for creator tools, analytics dashboards, and consumer apps for TikTok's audience.

Appwrite requests `user.info.basic`, the narrowest TikTok scope that returns an identity, and stores the user's display name and avatar. TikTok doesn't offer an email scope, so TikTok users are created without an email address. In your TikTok app's settings, the client ID is called the **client key**.

## Kakao

[Kakao Login](https://developers.kakao.com/docs/latest/en/kakaologin/common) signs users in with their Kakao account, the same one they use for KakaoTalk. For apps that serve users in South Korea, it's the sign-in option people expect to see.

Kakao asks users for the consent items you configure on your Kakao app, so Appwrite doesn't request any scopes by default. If you request a scope that isn't enabled there, Kakao rejects the sign-in with error `KOE205`. Appwrite stores the user's nickname and profile image, and their email when the user grants the email consent item. Appwrite treats that email as verified only when Kakao reports it as both verified and valid. In the Kakao developer console, the client ID is your app's **REST API key**.

## Webflow

[Webflow](https://developers.webflow.com/data/reference/oauth-app) is a visual website builder with a Data API and a marketplace for Webflow Apps. If you're building for Webflow site owners, Sign in with Webflow gives you the user and a Webflow API token in one step.

Appwrite requests `authorized_user:read` and stores the user's name and email. Add scopes such as `sites:read` to work with the user's sites. Webflow doesn't issue refresh tokens or report whether the email is verified, so Appwrite stores the email as unverified.

## The new providers at a glance

The last two columns show whether each provider shares the user's email address and profile photo with Appwrite, and whether Appwrite marks the email as verified.

| Provider    | Default scopes                            | Email address                                                                | Profile photo |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |
| Hugging Face | `openid`, `profile`, `email`              | Shared, verified if Hugging Face verified it                                 | Shared        |
| Resend       | None, pass `emails:send` or `full_access` | Not shared                                                                   | Not shared    |
| Cloudflare   | `user-details.read`, `offline_access`     | Shared, marked unverified                                                    | Not shared    |
| TikTok       | `user.info.basic`                         | Not shared                                                                   | Shared        |
| Kakao        | None, uses your app's consent items       | Shared if the user consents, verified if Kakao reports it verified and valid | Shared        |
| Webflow      | `authorized_user:read`                    | Shared, marked unverified                                                    | Not shared    |

The email column also decides how Appwrite matches a sign-in to an existing account:

- **Verified email.** When Hugging Face or Kakao reports the address as verified (for Kakao, both verified and valid), a sign-in whose email matches an existing user signs in to that user.
- **Unverified email.** Cloudflare and Webflow users get the email with `emailVerification` set to `false`, as does a Hugging Face or Kakao user whose email the provider hasn't verified. If another user already has that address, Appwrite redirects to your failure URL instead of signing the user in, because an unverified address doesn't prove ownership. To connect the provider to that account, have the user sign in first and then start `createOAuth2Session` from the browser, which links the provider as a new [identity](/docs/products/auth/identities). A sign-in your server starts with `createOAuth2Token` never links to the browser's signed-in user.
- **No email.** Appwrite creates TikTok and Resend users without an email address and recognizes them on later sign-ins by their provider user ID. If your app needs an email, ask for it after sign-in and set it from your server with the Users API's `updateEmail` method.

Providers that share a photo also power [Avatars user photos](/docs/products/avatars/user-photos), so a TikTok or Kakao user gets their real avatar in your app without another API call.

# Add the new providers to your app

Before you write any code:

1. In the Appwrite Console, open your project and go to **Auth** > **Settings**.
2. Open the provider, turn it on, and paste the client ID and secret from the provider's developer portal.
3. Copy the redirect URI shown in the modal into your app's settings on the provider's side.
4. Add your app's domain as a web platform so Appwrite accepts your `success` and `failure` URLs.

## Sign in from server-side apps

Server-side apps, including server-rendered (SSR) apps built with frameworks like Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit, and Astro, handle sign-in on the server and keep the session in a cookie. Server-side OAuth2 login takes two routes. The first calls `createOAuth2Token` and redirects the user to the provider. The second receives the user back from Appwrite, checks that this browser started the sign-in, and exchanges the `userId` and `secret` URL parameters for a session with `createSession`. The [server-side rendering docs](/docs/products/auth/server-side-rendering#oauth2) cover the full flow in more languages.

The examples below use Express and `node-appwrite` 29.4.0 or later, which includes all six `OAuthProvider` values. In a framework, the same two routes become route handlers or server endpoints. Start with an admin client that uses an API key with the `sessions.write` scope, and a function that stores a random `state` value in a cookie. Appwrite returns that value unchanged on your `success` and `failure` URLs, so the callback can check that the sign-in it receives is one this browser started.

```server-nodejs
import crypto from 'node:crypto';
import express from 'express';
import cookieParser from 'cookie-parser';
import { Client, Account, OAuthProvider } from 'node-appwrite';

const app = express();
app.use(cookieParser());

const adminClient = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>'); // Needs the sessions.write scope

// A random value only this browser holds
function createState(res) {
    const state = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');
    res.cookie('oauth_state', state, {
        httpOnly: true,
        secure: true,
        sameSite: 'lax', // Sent on the redirect back to your app
        maxAge: 10 * 60 * 1000,
        path: '/',
    });
    return state;
}
```

Next, add a route that starts the sign-in for each provider you enabled.

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #huggingface title="Hugging Face" %}

```server-nodejs
app.get('/oauth/huggingface', async (req, res) => {
    const account = new Account(adminClient);

    const redirectUrl = await account.createOAuth2Token({
        provider: OAuthProvider.Huggingface,
        success: 'https://example.com/oauth/success',
        failure: 'https://example.com/oauth/failure',
        state: createState(res),
    });

    res.redirect(redirectUrl);
});
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #resend title="Resend" %}

```server-nodejs
app.get('/oauth/resend', async (req, res) => {
    const account = new Account(adminClient);

    const redirectUrl = await account.createOAuth2Token({
        provider: OAuthProvider.Resend,
        success: 'https://example.com/oauth/success',
        failure: 'https://example.com/oauth/failure',
        scopes: ['emails:send'], // Resend needs an explicit scope
        state: createState(res),
    });

    res.redirect(redirectUrl);
});
```

{% /tabsitem %}

