To request more access, add a `scopes` array. Appwrite adds your scopes to the provider's defaults, so `scopes: ['inference-api']` on Hugging Face still signs the user in with `openid`, `profile`, and `email`.

Finally, add the callback route. It's the same for every provider: check that the `state` URL parameter matches the cookie, then create the session and store the session secret in a cookie. Without the `state` check, an attacker could send a user a success link for the attacker's account and sign the user into it.

```server-nodejs
app.get('/oauth/success', async (req, res) => {
    const { userId, secret, state } = req.query;

    // Only accept a sign-in this browser started
    const expected = req.cookies.oauth_state;
    if (!expected || state !== expected) {
        return res.redirect('/login?error=oauth');
    }
    res.clearCookie('oauth_state', { path: '/' });

    try {
        const account = new Account(adminClient);
        const session = await account.createSession({ userId, secret });

        res.cookie('a_session_<PROJECT_ID>', session.secret, {
            httpOnly: true,
            secure: true,
            sameSite: 'lax',
            expires: new Date(session.expire),
            path: '/',
        });

        res.redirect('/');
    } catch (error) {
        res.redirect('/login?error=oauth');
    }
});
```

From here, every request that needs the user creates a session client from that cookie, as shown in the [session client docs](/docs/products/auth/server-side-rendering#session-client).

## Sign in from the browser

If your app signs users in from the browser instead, call `createOAuth2Session` from the `appwrite` Web SDK 28.3.0 or later. Appwrite redirects the user to the provider, creates the session, and sends them back to your `success` URL.

```client-web
import { Client, Account, OAuthProvider } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const account = new Account(client);

account.createOAuth2Session({
    provider: OAuthProvider.Kakao, // Or any of the six new values
    success: 'https://example.com/success',
    failure: 'https://example.com/failure',
});
```

The same call works in the mobile and Flutter client SDKs with the matching `OAuthProvider` value.

# Getting started with the new OAuth2 providers

All six providers are live on Appwrite Cloud now. Enable the ones your users already have accounts on, pass the `OAuthProvider` value to `createOAuth2Token` in your sign-in route, and you're done. For self-hosted Appwrite, upgrade to 2.4 or later to get all six.

